<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> began on the night of 20–21 August 1968, when troops of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> entered the country to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> reforms. Resistance was largely non-violent, but more than a hundred civilians were killed. Soviet troops remained in Czechoslovakia until 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact-invasion-of-czechoslovakia.jpg" alt="Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Главцентроархив · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Invasion_of_Czechoslovakia_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>De-Stalinization in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> began under Antonín Novotný in the late 1950s and early 1960s but progressed more slowly than in most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> states. An economic downturn in the early 1960s, and the New Economic Model of 1965, increased demand for political reform. On 5 January 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a> was elected First Secretary of the Communist Party of Czechoslovakia (KSČ), beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a>. His reforms partly decentralised the economy and loosened restrictions on the media, speech and travel, and censorship was abolished on 4 March 1968.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-brezhnev">Leonid Brezhnev</a> and the leaders of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> countries feared that the liberalisation would harm their interests. They worried that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> might defect from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> without strategic depth or Czechoslovakia's industrial base in a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, although Czechoslovak leaders had no intention of leaving the Warsaw Pact. Negotiations in July 1968 at Čierna nad Tisou ended in a compromise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At about 11 pm on 20 August 1968 the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> invaded. That night 250,000 troops and 2,000 tanks entered the country, and the total eventually reached about 500,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and Albania refused to take part, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> ordered East German forces not to cross the border, because of the public memory of the German occupation three decades earlier. Brezhnev wanted the operation to appear multilateral, but Soviet troops outnumbered the others five to one, and the Soviet High Command was in charge throughout.</p><p>The Dubček government had taken no steps to forestall an invasion. By the morning of 21 August Dubček and other prominent reformists had been arrested and flown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Popular opposition took the form of spontaneous nonviolent resistance, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a> and Slovaks met the soldiers with arguments and reproaches. During the occupation 137 Czechoslovaks were killed and 500 seriously wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Reactions were divided. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> states supported the invasion, while Western nations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Albania condemned it, and many communist parties outside the bloc split or lost influence. Albania formally withdrew from the Warsaw Pact in September 1968. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-husak">Gustáv Husák</a> replaced Dubček as leader of the KSČ in April 1969, his regime began normalisation, restoring firm party rule and removing the reformers. In 1989 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> each condemned or apologised for their part in the invasion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
